      "content": "I apologize for the previous oversimplification. You’re correct in pointing out that the data on manufacturing jobs and wage stagnation after agreements like the TPP could suggest that for the domestic working class, the TPP is seen more critically, even potentially as a failure. The TPP has attracted criticism for contributing to job losses in sectors like manufacturing, which traditionally provide well-paying employment opportunities. Furthermore, wage stagnation since such trade agreements were brought into play has also been a point of contention. These economic patterns suggest that, without safeguard measures, the TPP could benefit a small minority – such as investors and politically connected business entities – while disadvantaging the general working-class population, especially in manufacturing industries. The argument for success in the TPP, when considering the working class, becomes much harder to defend and can likely be discounted when weighed against evidence of negative impacts on wages and employment.",
